speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, 1 also wish to present a petition.
To the honourable House of Commons of Canada and Parliament assembled, the petition of the undersigned residents of Canada in cities such as Blaine Lake, North Batt-leford, Krydor, etc., who now avail themselves of their old and inalienable rights to voice a common grievance, with the firm assurance that your honourable House will find a remedy.
They humbly declare that:
Whereas the farmers of Western Canada have paid $131 million to export their grain in the year 1981-82, in accordance with the Crow rate;
Whereas this will contribute $6.3 billion to Canada's trade balance;
Whereas the undersigned believe this is a fair contribution to the Canadian economy;
And whereas western farmers have no intention of paying the Crow rate more than once;
Wherefore, the petitioners humbly call upon Parliament to maintain the present statutory grain Crow rate.
And as in duty bound your petitioners will ever pray.

mr. Mcknight-increase in st. Lawrence seaway tolls

Mr. Speaker, 1 wish to present a petition.
The petition of the undersigned residents of Canada who now exercise their ancient and undisputed right to present a grievance with the full assurance that the House will remedy it.
Humbly sheweth that:
Whereas Western Canadian farmers paid $131 million to move export grain under the Crow freight rate in the 1981-82 crop year;
Whereas this grain in turn contributed $6.3 billion to the balance of trade;
Whereas the undersigned believe that this is the farmers' fair contribution to the Canadian economy;
And whereas Western farmers will pay one times Crow;
October 27, 1983

Wherefore the undersigned, your petitioners, humbly pray and call upon Parliament to maintain the present statutory grain Crow rate,
And as in duty bound your petitioners will ever pray. [English]
